# SYNOPSIS

```text
cs [-f file]
dns [-f file]

/net/cs
/net/dns
```

# DESCRIPTION

cs and dns are Plan 9's, as services: cs translates dial strings into where to call, and dns looks names up in the domain name system for it.
Each serves one file at `net` in its tree, which a manifest mounts after the stack's files (ip(4)):

```text
use net
use cs
use dns
mount /net net/net after
mount /net cs/net after
mount /net dns/net after
```

dial(2) asks `/net/cs`; programs rarely open either file themselves.
Both read the network database (ndb(6)), `/lib/ndb/local` or the `-f` file, through their own namespace, as they reach the stack's `/net`.

Each file is asked the same way: a write is one query, and each read after it takes one answer, until a read returns nothing.
The write waits until the query is answered, and fails if it has no answer.
A channel's queries are its own, and each is answered on a thread of its own, so a slow one holds up no other.
Writing `refresh` to either reads the database's files again; dns also drops what it has kept.

## cs

A query is a dial string, `[`<network>`!]`<host>`!`<service>, and each answer a clone file and what to write to its conversation's `ctl` after `connect` or `announce`:

```rc
% echo -n tcp!qemu-echo!echo >[1=0] ... /net/cs
/net/tcp/clone 10.0.2.100!7
```

The network `net` is each network `/net` has that a call may use: `tcp`.
For `tcp`, `udp`, `icmp` and `icmpv6`, cs translates the host and the service; any other network's are passed on as they are (`/net/ether0/clone ff!0806`).
A fourth field is passed on after the port.

The host is
: an address
  IPv4 or IPv6, used as it is; `[`<address>`]` too.
: `*`
  Any address of ours, for `announce`: the answer is the port alone, `/net/tcp/clone 7`.
: `$`<attr>
  Each value of <attr> the database gives our addresses (ndb(6)), looked up in turn: `tcp!$auth!rcpu`.
: a domain name
  One with a dot: asked of dns first, then looked up in the database (`dom=`).
: any other name
  Looked up in the database (`sys=`); failing that, its `dom` is asked of dns, then the name itself.

The service is a port, `*`, or a name the database gives a port for the network (`tcp=echo port=7`).

An address of a version this machine has none of is left out, and one on a network of ours comes first.
At most 20 answers are given.
dns is asked for IPv4's addresses (`ip`) and, if this machine has an IPv6 address that isn't link-local, IPv6's (`ipv6`).

A query beginning `!` asks the database itself.
`!`<attr>`=`<value> [<attr>`=`<value> ...] answers with the lines of the first entry that has every pair, a value `*` matching any; `!ipinfo` [<attr>`=`<value>] <attr> ... answers with the attributes' values for an address, ours if no pair is given, on one line.

The write fails `notFound` when the host can't be translated or nothing matches, `invalid` when the service can't be or the query is malformed, and `io` when dns failed; dial(2) says `can't translate address`, `can't translate service` and `temporary problem: dns failure`.

## dns

A query is a name and a type, `plan9.io ip`, and each answer one record, its owner, type, a tab and its data:

```text
plan9.io ip	45.33.21.187
```

A query beginning `!` reads the records as ndb pairs instead, `dom=plan9.io ip=45.33.21.187`, as cs asks.
A name ending in a dot has it dropped; an address asked for its `ptr` is asked by its reverse name (`15.2.0.10.in-addr.arpa`).

The types are `ip` (A), `ipv6` (AAAA), `cname`, `ns`, `mx`, `ptr`, `soa`, `txt`, `hinfo`, `srv`, `all` (or `any`), and any type's number.
A CNAME is followed: the records answer for the name it leads to.

dns is a resolver: it asks the name servers `/net/ndb` gives, then those the database gives our addresses (`dns=`), to recurse for it.
Each is asked in turn, three rounds, waiting 1, 2 and then 4 seconds; a reply that is truncated is asked again over TCP.
Records are kept for their TTL, a day at most; a name that doesn't exist, or has no records of the type, is kept as such for its zone's SOA minimum, or a minute.

The write fails `notFound` when the name doesn't exist (`name does not exist`) or has no records of the type (`resource does not exist`), `io` when no server answered or every one failed (`dns failure`), and `invalid` when the query is malformed or its type unknown.

# EXAMPLES

A dial by name:

```swift
var c = try ns.dial("tcp!qemu-echo!echo")
```

# FILES

: `/svc/cs/net`
  cs's file, `cs`, to mount after the stack's at `/net`.
: `/svc/cs/status`
  The database's file.
: `/svc/dns/net`
  dns's file, `dns`, to mount after the stack's at `/net`.
: `/svc/dns/status`
  The name servers dns would ask now, or `none`.

